<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prince Marko</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1335–1395</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Kraljević Marko, Marko Mrnjavčević</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prince Marko, Marko Mrnjavčević, was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vukasin-mrnjavcevic">King Vukašin</a> and ruled a realm around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prilep">Prilep</a> in Macedonia after his father's death at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maritsa">Battle of the Maritsa</a> in 1371. He became an Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> and was killed fighting for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mircea-the-elder">Mircea the Elder of Wallachia</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rovine">Battle of Rovine</a> in 1395. As Kraljević Marko he is the best-loved hero of South Slavic epic poetry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prince-marko.jpg" alt="Prince Marko" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marko_Mrnjavcevic.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
